        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p13-q14-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "26 rolls.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p13-q14-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use logarithms to find the smallest whole number of rolls for which the probability of no 5 is below ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "0.01",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of rolls. The probability of missing 5 on all $n$ rolls must be less than $0.01$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} 1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n &> 0.99 \\\\ \\left(\\frac56\\right)^n &< 0.01 \\\\ n\\log\\left(\\frac56\\right) &< \\log(0.01) \\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $\\log\\left(\\frac56\\right)<0$, dividing reverses the inequality.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} n &> \\frac{\\log(0.01)}{\\log\\left(\\frac56\\right)} \\end{align*}$$\n\nThe bound lies between $25$ and $26$, so the minimum is $26$ rolls.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      }
